    "doi": "https://doi.org/10.5194/acp-15-2791-2015",
    "abstract": "Abstract. The HadGEM2 earth system climate model was used to assess the impact of biomass burning on surface ozone concentrations over the Amazon forest and its impact on vegetation, under present-day climate conditions. Here we consider biomass burning emissions from wildfires, deforestation fires, agricultural forest burning, and residential and commercial combustion. Simulated surface ozone concentration is evaluated against observations taken at two sites in the Brazilian Amazon forest for years 2010 to 2012. The model is able to reproduce the observed diurnal cycle of surface ozone mixing ratio at the two sites, but overestimates the magnitude of the monthly averaged hourly measurements by 5\u201315 ppb for each available month at one of the sites. We vary biomass burning emissions over South America by \u00b120, 40, 60, 80 and 100% to quantify the modelled impact of biomass burning on surface ozone concentrations and ozone damage on vegetation productivity over the Amazon forest. We used the ozone damage scheme in the \"high\" sensitivity mode to give an upper limit for this effect. Decreasing South American biomass burning emissions by 100% (i.e. to zero) reduces surface ozone concentrations (by about 15 ppb during the biomass burning season) and suggests a 15% increase in monthly mean net primary productivity averaged over the Amazon forest, with local increases up to 60%. The simulated impact of ozone damage from present-day biomass burning on vegetation productivity is about 230 TgC yr\u22121. Taking into account that uncertainty in these estimates is substantial, this ozone damage impact over the Amazon forest is of the same order of magnitude as the release of carbon dioxide due to fire in South America; in effect it potentially doubles the impact of biomass burning on the carbon cycle.",
    "field": "Atmosphere-biosphere interactions",
    "problem": "The study addresses the potential underestimation of biomass burning's impact on the Amazon carbon cycle by using a coupled climate model to quantify how resulting ozone pollution damages vegetation productivity, an effect which could be as significant as the direct carbon dioxide emissions from the fires themselves.",
    "method": "This study uses the HadGEM2 earth system model to conduct a sensitivity analysis, systematically varying South American biomass burning emissions by \u00b120% to \u00b1100% to quantify the impact on surface ozone concentrations and vegetation productivity over the Amazon forest.",
    "task": "The task is to use the HadGEM2 climate model to assess the impact of varying biomass burning emissions on surface ozone concentrations and resulting damage to vegetation productivity over the Amazon forest.",
